==English==
===Verb===
{{head|en|verb|head=[[keep]] the [[wolf]] from the [[door]]}}

# {{label|en|idiomatic}} To [[ward off]] [[poverty]] or [[hunger]].
#: ''They didn't earn much, but it was enough to '''keep the wolf from the door'''.''
# {{label|en|idiomatic}} {{rfv-sense}} To delay sexual [[ejaculation]].

===Etymology===
The original saying may have been ''keep the wolf from the gate'' and dates from at least 1470.<ref>John Hardyng, Chronicle, XCVIII, xii, 1470 (republished in 1812)</ref>
By the 1500's the saying had become ''keep the wolf from the door''. The meaning remained unchanged from its current usage.<ref>Vol 3 of familiar letters, James Howell, publisher Humphrey Moseley, St Pauls Churchyard, 1655, page 8, copy of letter from 1647</ref>

There is a suggestion that the phrase may have originated or been derived from French or German phrases. The German ''Wolfsmagen'' directly translated is ''wolfs stomach'' and means ''a keen appetite'' and the French ''manger comme un loup'' means ''eat like a wolf''.<ref>Notes and Queries, 2nd series vol 4, no 84, 8 August 1857, page 115, Bell and Dalby, Fleet Street</ref>

